IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `jccv-core`
IMPORT `jccv-seat`

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (SA) -- cases for the party's seat (jccv-seat.l4), with a few on
-- jccv-core.l4 functions the seat composes (s 3(1) State matter, s 6A(1)). Every expected value was worked by hand from
-- the deposited text (the section is named on each group), never copied from the encoding's output. The Act sets no
-- numbers or dates; the edges here are the order in which the text's conditions displace one another (criminal before
-- court, special federal matter appeal before order before s 6A(4), convenience before special reasons, notice before
-- time) and each limb of every "or" and "and".
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: the party`

`SA SC` MEANS `the Supreme Court of South Australia`
`other SC` MEANS `the Supreme Court of another State, the Northern Territory or the ACT`
`external SC` MEANS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
`WA family` MEANS `the State Family Court of another State (the Family Court of Western Australia)`
`FCA` MEANS `the Federal Court of Australia`
`FamCA` MEANS `the Family Court of Australia`
`no s 5 view` MEANS `no: the court has not formed the view that s 5 requires, so s 5 does not require a transfer`

§§ `ss 3(1), 3A, 4(5): coverage`

#ASSERT `Does the cross-vesting scheme cover my proceeding` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: your proceeding is a civil proceeding, which the cross-vesting scheme covers (s 3(1))`
#ASSERT `Does the cross-vesting scheme cover my proceeding` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act, and the Act gives no court criminal jurisdiction (ss 3(1), 4(5))`
#ASSERT `Does the cross-vesting scheme cover my proceeding` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Act does not apply to the jurisdiction dealt with by Division 1 of Part 9.6A of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth) (s 3A)`
-- both: s 3(1) is the definition read first; criminal wins.
#ASSERT `Does the cross-vesting scheme cover my proceeding` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a criminal proceeding is not a proceeding under this Act, and the Act gives no court criminal jurisdiction (ss 3(1), 4(5))`

§§ `s 3(1) State matter: (a) or (b)`

#ASSERT `is a State matter (s 3(1))` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `is a State matter (s 3(1))` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `is a State matter (s 3(1))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a State matter (s 3(1))` FALSE FALSE))

§§ `s 4: which courts may hear a South Australian State matter`

-- A negligence claim the SA Supreme Court could hear under SA law, asked of each court.
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `other SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `external SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `WA family` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the State Family Court of another State has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(4))`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `SA SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 4 is not needed: the Supreme Court of South Australia has jurisdiction in a State matter of its own (s 3(1))`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `FCA` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 4 now vests jurisdiction only in other States' and Territories' Supreme Courts and State Family Courts (its subsections (1) and (2) were deleted in 2001); this Act does not say`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `FamCA` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 4 now vests jurisdiction only in other States' and Territories' Supreme Courts and State Family Courts (its subsections (1) and (2) were deleted in 2001); this Act does not say`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `a court of South Australia other than the Supreme Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 4 now vests jurisdiction only in other States' and Territories' Supreme Courts and State Family Courts (its subsections (1) and (2) were deleted in 2001); this Act does not say`
-- (b) limb: removed under s 8, though the Supreme Court had no jurisdiction of its own.
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `other SC` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in your South Australian matter (s 4(3))`
-- neither limb.
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `other SC` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: your matter is not a State matter, so s 4 vests no jurisdiction in it (ss 3(1), 4)`
-- criminal: s 4(5), even for a court s 4(3) names.
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `other SC` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 4 vests no jurisdiction with respect to criminal matters (s 4(5))`
#ASSERT `Can that court hear my South Australian claim` `other SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Act does not apply to Corporations Act Part 9.6A Division 1 jurisdiction (s 3A)`

§§ `s 5(1): SA Supreme Court to the Federal Court or Family Court`

#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to the Federal Court or Family Court` TRUE `the Federal Court` FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Federal Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to the Federal Court or Family Court` TRUE `the Family Court` FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Family Court (s 5(1))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE `the Federal Court` FALSE EQUALS `no s 5 view`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to the Federal Court or Family Court` TRUE `the Federal Court` TRUE EQUALS `not required: s 5(1) does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
-- s 6A(4) matters only once s 5(1) would otherwise bite.
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE `the Family Court` TRUE EQUALS `no s 5 view`

§§ `s 5(2): SA Supreme Court to another Supreme Court`

#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to another Supreme Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to another Supreme Court` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to another Supreme Court` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court in the interests of justice (s 5(2)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to another Supreme Court` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the court must transfer your proceeding to the other Supreme Court, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(2)(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must my SA Supreme Court proceeding go to another Supreme Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no s 5 view`

§§ `s 5(3): another Supreme Court to the SA Supreme Court`

#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from another Supreme Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(3)(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from another Supreme Court` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from another Supreme Court` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the other Supreme Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(3)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from another Supreme Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no s 5 view`

§§ `s 5(4), s 6A(2), (3): Federal Court or Family Court to the SA Supreme Court`

#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as it arises out of or is related to a proceeding there and that court is more appropriate (s 5(4)(b)(i))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia, as more appropriate having regard to where it could be brought, the law it turns on and the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Federal Court or Family Court must transfer your proceeding to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (s 5(4)(b)(iii))`
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from the Federal Court or Family Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no s 5 view`
-- s 6A(3): s 5(4) does not apply to a federal matter proceeding, even where its grounds would be met.
#ASSERT `Must my proceeding come to the SA Supreme Court from the Federal Court or Family Court` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 5(4) does not apply to a federal matter proceeding; the Federal Court or Family Court may still transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice (ss 6A(2), (3))`

§§ `s 5(6): a related proceeding follows`

#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the first court must transfer your related proceeding to the same court as the one it transferred (s 5(6))`
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 5(6) applies only where the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law, yours is related to it and pending in that court, and it finds the interests of justice favour the other court`
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 5(6) applies only where the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law, yours is related to it and pending in that court, and it finds the interests of justice favour the other court`
#ASSERT `Must my related proceeding follow the transferred one` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 5(6) applies only where the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law, yours is related to it and pending in that court, and it finds the interests of justice favour the other court`

§§ `ss 3(1), 5(7): who may apply`

#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer` `a party to the proceeding` EQUALS `yes: as a party you may apply for the transfer; the court may also act on its own initiative (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer` `a person who has intervened in the proceeding` EQUALS `yes: an intervener is a party, so you may apply for the transfer (ss 3(1), 5(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer` `the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory` EQUALS `yes: an Attorney-General may apply for the transfer (s 5(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a transfer` `someone else` EQUALS `the Act gives you no application: s 5(7) names a party, an Attorney-General and the court of its own initiative; the court may still transfer on its own initiative`

§§ `s 5(8): the lawyer follows the proceeding`

#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act in the new court` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: your lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court in this proceeding as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act in the new court` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 5(8) does not help: it carries over only the entitlement of a barrister or solicitor entitled to practise in the court the proceeding left, and only for the transferred proceeding and a related one determined with it`
#ASSERT `Can my lawyer act in the new court` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 5(8) does not help: it carries over only the entitlement of a barrister or solicitor entitled to practise in the court the proceeding left, and only for the transferred proceeding and a related one determined with it`

§§ `s 6: special federal matters`

#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `not a special federal matter` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 6 does not apply: no matter for determination is a special federal matter`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `a special federal matter under paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within the Federal Court's jurisdiction (including its accrued jurisdiction) to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `a special federal matter under paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court must transfer so much of your proceeding as is within that court's jurisdiction to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it considers appropriate (s 6(1), (1a), (2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `a special federal matter under paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the Supreme Court keeps it: it has ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the proceeding`
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `a special federal matter under paragraph (a), (b), (c), (d) or (e) of the Commonwealth definition` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not required: s 6 does not require the Supreme Court to transfer a federal matter proceeding while the related State matter proceeding is pending in a South Australian court, but it may if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4))`
-- s 6(9): the appeal is outside s 6 altogether, whatever else is true.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `a special federal matter under paragraph (ab) of the Commonwealth definition` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 6 does not apply: an appeal in the Full Court where the court below ordered under s 6(3) that it determine the special federal matter (s 6(9))`
-- not a special federal matter: nothing else matters.
#ASSERT `Must my special federal matter be transferred` `not a special federal matter` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 6 does not apply: no matter for determination is a special federal matter`

§§ `s 6(3)-(6): keeping a special federal matter`

#ASSERT `Can the SA Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Supreme Court may order that it determine your proceeding itself (s 6(3), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can the SA Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: a written notice of the nature of the special federal matter must first be given to the Commonwealth and South Australian Attorneys-General; the court may adjourn and may direct a party to give it (s 6(4)(a), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can the SA Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: a reasonable time must first pass after the notice for the Attorneys-General to consider making submissions; the court may adjourn for it (s 6(4)(b), (5)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can the SA Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the court may keep it only if satisfied that there are special reasons in the particular circumstances (s 6(3))`
-- convenience only: refused even though the court says it is satisfied and the notice steps are done.
#ASSERT `Can the SA Supreme Court keep my special federal matter` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: reasons relevant to the convenience of the parties are not special reasons (s 6(3))`

§§ `s 6(7): urgent interlocutory relief`

#ASSERT `Can I get urgent interim relief meanwhile` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: nothing in s 6 stops the Supreme Court granting urgent interlocutory relief in the interests of justice (s 6(7))`
#ASSERT `Can I get urgent interim relief meanwhile` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 6(7) does not help: it saves only urgent relief of an interlocutory nature that is in the interests of justice; this Act does not say more`
#ASSERT `Can I get urgent interim relief meanwhile` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 6(7) does not help: it saves only urgent relief of an interlocutory nature that is in the interests of justice; this Act does not say more`

§§ `s 6A(1): a federal matter proceeding needs every limb`

#ASSERT `is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a federal matter proceeding (s 6A(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE))

§§ `s 6A(2)-(4): what follows`

#ASSERT `Is mine a federal matter proceeding and what follows` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `FCA` EQUALS `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Federal Court or Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice, including the desirability of related proceedings being heard together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Is mine a federal matter proceeding and what follows` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `FamCA` EQUALS `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Federal Court or Family Court may transfer it to the Supreme Court of South Australia in the interests of justice, including the desirability of related proceedings being heard together; s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Is mine a federal matter proceeding and what follows` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `SA SC` EQUALS `yes, a federal matter proceeding: the Supreme Court of South Australia need not transfer it to the Federal Court or Family Court under s 5(1) or s 6, but may if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4))`
#ASSERT `Is mine a federal matter proceeding and what follows` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `other SC` EQUALS `yes, a federal matter proceeding, but s 6A says what follows only while it is in the Federal Court, the Family Court or the Supreme Court of South Australia`
#ASSERT `Is mine a federal matter proceeding and what follows` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE `FCA` EQUALS `no: s 6A applies only to a proceeding that meets all of s 6A(1)(a), (b) and (c)`

§§ `ss 6(8), 7(7): decisions not invalidated`

#ASSERT `Is the decision in my case invalid` `the Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or the s 6(4) notice, through inadvertence` EQUALS `no: nothing in s 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision in my case invalid` `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, having found the interests of justice required it to` EQUALS `no: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision in my case invalid` `the Full Court of the Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends elsewhere, through inadvertence` EQUALS `no: nothing in s 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `Is the decision in my case invalid` `something else` EQUALS `not covered: ss 6(8) and 7(7) save only those decisions; this Act does not say`

§§ `s 7(1)-(5): appeal routes`

#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a single judge of the Supreme Court of South Australia` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, and only there (s 7(3))`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a single judge of the Supreme Court of South Australia` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court, and only there (s 7(4)), subject to s 7(6) and (7)`
-- one Schedule Act matter among others is enough for s 7(4) ("a matter"); s 7(3) needs the "only matters" to be non-Schedule.
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a single judge of the Supreme Court of South Australia` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court, and only there (s 7(4)), subject to s 7(6) and (7)`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no appeal lies from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, nor from one of those courts to the other (s 7(1), (2)); this Act does not say where it goes`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no appeal lies from a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the Full Court of the Supreme Court of South Australia, nor from one of those courts to the other (s 7(1), (2)); this Act does not say where it goes`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a judge of a State Family Court` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(5))`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a judge of a State Family Court` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `a judge of a State Family Court` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`
#ASSERT `Where does my appeal go` `another court` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: s 7 does not say where this appeal goes`

§§ `s 7(6): the Full Court finds a Schedule Act matter part way`

#ASSERT `Will the Full Court send my appeal on` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Full Court must transfer your appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(6))`
#ASSERT `Will the Full Court send my appeal on` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the interests of justice require the Full Court to go on and decide your appeal itself (s 7(6)); its decision is not invalid (s 7(7))`
#ASSERT `Will the Full Court send my appeal on` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 7(6) applies only where the Full Court has begun hearing the appeal and it then appears that s 7(4) applies`
#ASSERT `Will the Full Court send my appeal on` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 7(6) applies only where the Full Court has begun hearing the appeal and it then appears that s 7(4) applies`

§§ `s 13: no appeal from a transfer decision`

#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision` `a decision to transfer or remove the proceeding, or not to, under this Act` EQUALS `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision` `a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)` EQUALS `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision on which rules of evidence and procedure apply (s 13(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I appeal that decision` `another decision` EQUALS `s 13 does not bar it; whether an appeal lies is for the general law and other sections (s 7 on routes)`

§§ `s 8: removal into the Supreme Court`

#ASSERT `Can my proceeding be removed into the SA Supreme Court` `a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may apply (or the Supreme Court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may remove your proceeding to itself; the Act then applies as if it were pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8)`
#ASSERT `Can my proceeding be removed into the SA Supreme Court` `a tribunal established by or under an Act` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may apply (or the Supreme Court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may remove your proceeding to itself; the Act then applies as if it were pending there, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8)`
#ASSERT `Can my proceeding be removed into the SA Supreme Court` `a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Supreme Court may remove it only where it appears to that court that it is related to a proceeding in the Federal Court, the Family Court or another Supreme Court that could then be transferred to it, or that removal is needed to consider a transfer (s 8(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my proceeding be removed into the SA Supreme Court` `somewhere else` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 8 reaches only a proceeding pending in a South Australian court other than the Supreme Court, or in a tribunal established by or under an Act`

§§ `s 10: Trade Practices Act Part V matters sent down`

#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may apply (or the court may act of its own initiative) and the Supreme Court may transfer your proceeding to the South Australian court that has jurisdiction over all of it (s 10)`
#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`
#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`
#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`
#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`
#ASSERT `Can my Trade Practices claim go to a lower SA court` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 10 needs every one of its conditions: pending in a Supreme Court, a matter under Division 1 or 1A of Part V of the Trade Practices Act 1974, no special federal matter, not an appeal, and a lower South Australian court with jurisdiction over all the matters`

§§ `s 11(1): the law applied`

#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the written and unwritten law of the State or Territory under whose written law your right of action arises; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(b), (c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules; rules of evidence and procedure as the court considers appropriate, from those of a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(a), (c))`
#ASSERT `Which law applies to my claim` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 11(1) does not direct the court: it applies only where the court will, or is likely to, be exercising cross-vested jurisdiction`

§§ `s 11(3): steps already taken`

#ASSERT `Do the steps already taken still count` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the new court deals with your proceeding as if the steps taken in the old court, including its orders, had been taken there (s 11(3))`
#ASSERT `Do the steps already taken still count` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, except as the new court has ordered otherwise (s 11(3))`
#ASSERT `Do the steps already taken still count` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 11(3) does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`

§§ `s 12: costs before the transfer`

#ASSERT `Can the new court order the earlier costs` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the court your proceeding went to may order costs for the conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`
#ASSERT `Can the new court order the earlier costs` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 12 reaches only costs that another court has not already dealt with`
#ASSERT `Can the new court order the earlier costs` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 12 does not apply: your proceeding has not been transferred or removed`

§§ `s 14: enforcement and references`

#ASSERT `Can I enforce the judgment in South Australia` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: it is enforceable in South Australia as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce the judgment in South Australia` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 14(2) is not needed: a Supreme Court of South Australia judgment given in its own jurisdiction is enforceable as such`
#ASSERT `Can I enforce the judgment in South Australia` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 14(2) speaks only of judgments of the Supreme Court of South Australia`
#ASSERT `Does the other court's act count under SA law` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the South Australian law's reference to the Supreme Court is read as a reference to the court that did it (s 14(3))`
#ASSERT `Does the other court's act count under SA law` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not under s 14(3): it excludes laws relating to the enforcement of judgments (s 14(2) deals with enforcement)`
#ASSERT `Does the other court's act count under SA law` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 14(3) does not apply: the thing was not done by another court in jurisdiction conferred by this Act`
